course: biology
lesson: 212
title: Chickens: eggs, pecking order and living dinosaurs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Look closely at a chicken's scaly feet, sharp claws and beady eye as it stalks across the yard, and you are looking at a living dinosaur.
explain: Birds are the one group of dinosaurs that survived, and the chicken shares many bones and features with its huge extinct relatives such as T. rex.
explain: Chickens were tamed from a wild bird of Asian forests, the red junglefowl, which lays only a few dozen eggs a year, but today's farm hens lay almost one every day, with or without a rooster.
explain: A flock is a little society with a pecking order, more than twenty different calls and mothers that talk to their chicks before they even hatch.
fact: A hen lays eggs with or without a rooster; shop eggs are not fertilised, because no rooster mated with the hen, so they could never hatch.
fact: Modern hens can lay around three hundred eggs a year, while wild junglefowl lay only a few dozen.
fact: Roosters crow at many times of day, not only at dawn, to claim their territory, and a study reported that a rooster partly closes its ear canals when crowing so it does not deafen itself.
fact: Chickens scratch and peck for seeds, worms and insects; they are omnivores and will even eat mice and small frogs.
fact: A flock has a pecking order: the top hen eats first and pecks lower hens, and the phrase «pecking order» for people comes from chickens.
fact: A broody hen, one that wants to sit on eggs, keeps her eggs warm for about three weeks, and before hatching the chicks peep inside the shell and the hen clucks back.
fact: Chickens bathe in dry dust, which cleans their feathers and gets rid of mites, tiny biting bugs, and a dust-bathing hen can look half dead though she is perfectly happy.
fact: Birds are living dinosaurs, and in 2007 scientists reported that proteins from a T. rex fossil were close to chicken proteins, though some still debate those results.
fact: Foxes hunt chickens, which cannot fly well and sleep deeply at night, so a hen house must be shut every evening.
fact: A chicken's eyes are on the sides of its head; it turns its head to look with one eye and then the other, and it sees more colours than we do, including ultraviolet.
fact: Chickens make more than twenty calls, with different alarms for danger from the air and from the ground, and a hen has a special call to tell her chicks she has found food.
fact: Shell colour, white, brown or even blue, depends on the breed and often matches the hen's earlobes, but the egg inside is the same.
exercise: Does a hen need a rooster to lay eggs? | No, hens lay without a rooster | Yes, always, or she cannot lay any eggs at all | Only for brown eggs | Only in spring
exercise: Can an egg from a supermarket hatch into a chick? | No, shop eggs are not fertilised | Yes, if you keep it warm in the airing cupboard for three weeks | Yes, but only brown ones | Only if a rooster sits on it
exercise: How many eggs can a modern hen lay in a year? | Around three hundred | About ten | Exactly one a week | Over five thousand
exercise: When do roosters crow? | At many times of day, to claim territory | Only at sunrise, to wake the farm up every morning | Only when it rains | Only at midnight
exercise: How does a rooster avoid deafening itself? | It partly closes its ear canals while crowing | It covers its ears tightly with its wings every time it crows | It has no ears at all | It crows only very quietly
exercise: What are chickens looking for when they scratch the ground? | Seeds, worms and insects | Eggs that other hens have lost | Water hidden deep underground below the yard | Their own fallen feathers
exercise: What kind of eaters are chickens? | Omnivores that even eat mice and small frogs | Only grain eaters, like wheat, corn and barley | Only grass eaters | Only meat eaters
exercise: What is a pecking order? | A ranking where the top hen eats first and pecks lower hens | The order in which the eggs are laid, the oldest hen laying first each morning | A list the farmer writes each day | The way chicks hatch one by one
exercise: How long does a broody hen sit on her eggs? | About three weeks | About three long days | About three months | About one short hour
exercise: How do a hen and her chicks talk before hatching? | The chicks peep inside the shell and the hen clucks back | They tap messages to each other on the shell, like a secret code | They cannot, chicks are silent | The hen sings and the chicks dance
exercise: Why do chickens take dust baths? | To clean their feathers and get rid of mites | To get warm in winter | To hide from foxes by covering themselves in dust from head to toe | To make their eggs brown
exercise: Which animals are birds such as chickens related to? | Dinosaurs | Frogs | Fish | Bats
exercise: Why must a hen house be shut every evening? | Foxes hunt chickens, which sleep deeply at night | Chickens wander off to the shops in the night if the door is left open | The eggs freeze if it is open | Hens hate seeing the Moon
exercise: Why does a chicken look at you with one eye and then the other? | Its eyes are on the sides of its head | It is blind in one eye | It is trying to wink at you in a friendly way | It cannot move its eyes at all
exercise: What can chickens see that we cannot? | Ultraviolet colours | Things through walls | Sounds as bright colours | Pictures in the dark
exercise: Why do chickens have different alarm calls? | To warn of danger from the air or from the ground | To tell the farmer it is time to eat | To say which hen is the oldest | They do not, all their calls are the same
exercise: What decides the colour of an eggshell? | The breed of hen | What the hen ate that day | The weather that week | The rooster's colour
exercise: Does a brown egg taste different from a white one? | No, the inside is the same | Yes, brown eggs are sweeter | Yes, white eggs are saltier | Only on Sundays, for a treat
test: In Chicken Run the hens build a plane and fly over the fence. What is true of real chickens? | They can only flap a short way, so a fox can catch them at night | They fly long distances like geese, in a V across the sky in autumn | They cannot move their wings at all | They fly only at night
test: «5 hens and a rooster, how many legs then» | 12 | 24 | 10 | 6
test: «4 chickens, 2 foxes sneaking round, count the tails» | 6 | 4 | 2 | 8
test: «our rooster crowing at 3 in the afternoon, it's broken innit» What is right? | Roosters crow at many times of day to claim territory | Roosters crow only at dawn, so he must be ill and needs a vet | He thinks it is morning | He is telling time wrong on purpose
test: «gonna hatch a chick from a supermarket egg in my airing cupboard» What will happen? | Nothing, shop eggs are not fertilised | A chick will hatch in three weeks | A dinosaur will hatch | It will hatch only if the egg is brown
test: «hen's lying on her side in the dust flapping, she's dying!!» What is likely? | She is happily dust bathing | She is having a heart attack | She is laying ten eggs at once | She is trying to dig to Australia
test: «brown eggs r healthier than white ones, mum says» What is right? | The shell colour comes from the breed; the egg inside is the same | Brown eggs have twice the goodness, because the brown shell is thicker and stronger | White eggs are bleached brown eggs | Blue eggs are poisonous
test: What would happen if a farmer forgot to shut the hen house at night where foxes live? | A fox could get in and kill the hens | Nothing, foxes are scared of hens | The hens would fly away safely | The hens would lay more eggs
test: «chickens r just vegetarian, they eat seeds» What is right? | They are omnivores and eat insects, worms and even mice | Yes, chickens eat only seeds and the odd bit of bread from the kitchen | They eat only grass like cows | They eat only meat
test: «how's a chicken a dinosaur?? it's tiny lol» What is right? | Birds are the dinosaurs that survived, and chickens share many of their features | It is not, birds came from fish | Only big birds are dinosaurs | Chickens are dinosaurs only in films
test: «that hen at the top pecks all the others, she's just mean» What is happening? | It is the pecking order: she is top hen and eats first | She is ill and confused | She is teaching them to read | She is cleaning their feathers, because the others cannot reach their own backs
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the laying of eggs by the domestic hen? | Eggs are laid irrespective of the presence of a rooster, those so laid being incapable of hatching | Eggs are laid solely in the presence of a rooster, a hen kept alone ceasing to lay altogether | All eggs so laid will hatch if warmed | The hen lays but a few eggs over her lifetime
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the domestic fowl must be enclosed at nightfall. | Foxes hunt chickens, which fly poorly and sleep deeply at night | Chickens are inclined to migrate after dark | Exposure to moonlight prevents laying | The eggs freeze unless the house is closed
exam: It is given that a hen is observed rolling in dry earth with outstretched wings. Ascertain the correct interpretation. | The hen is dust bathing to clean her feathers and remove mites | The hen is gravely ill and near death | The hen is constructing a nest underground for the eggs she will lay in spring | The hen is endeavouring to take flight
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the colouration of eggshells. | Shell colour is determined by breed and leaves the contents unaltered | Brown shells denote superior nourishment | White shells are produced by bleaching | Shell colour is determined by the weather
